# Git commit messages

When creating commits in this repo, follow these rules.

## Format

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(optional-scope): <short summary>

[optional body]

[optional footer]
```

- Language: **English only**
- Summary: imperative mood, ~50 characters, no trailing period
- Focus on **why**, not a file dump of **what**
- Body only when needed (context, trade-offs, breaking changes)
- One logical change per commit

## Types

| Type | Use for |
|------|---------|
| `feat` | New user-facing capability |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `refactor` | Internal change, no behavior change |
| `docs` | README, comments, examples only |
| `style` | Formatting, no logic change |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |
| `test` | Tests only |
| `build` | Cargo/npm/Tauri build or bundling |
| `ci` | CI config |
| `chore` | Maintenance that does not fit above |

Scopes (optional, lowercase): `ui`, `config`, `hotkey`, `launch`, `apps`, `tauri`, `deps`

## Examples

```
feat(hotkey): register configurable global shortcut

fix(launch): open Linux .desktop entries via gtk-launch

docs: document default hotkey and Accessibility setup

chore(deps): pin vite to patched 6.4.3
```

## Releases

Version bumps and git tags follow `.cursor/rules/versioning.mdc`.
Release commits use `chore:` (e.g. `chore: bump version to 0.2.0`).

## Do not

- Vague messages: `update`, `fix stuff`, `wip`, `changes`
- Commit secrets, personal configs, or private URLs
- Mix unrelated changes in one commit
- Use `--no-verify` unless the user explicitly asks
- Amend pushed commits unless the user explicitly asks

